E5 Target Publish Admission type/body/tuple, source application and ACK uncertainty

Original body/rejection source lock: main@28ddb7d40b07f9c2750369e4176ffdeb529a9095

The closed type-5 PUBLISH_ADMISSION body and shared System Mutation body v2 tuple remain unchanged. Type 13
TARGET_PUBLISH_ADMISSION carries its own operation body version 3 and requires the exact activated tuple
(1,1,SYSTEM_MUTATION,1,3). The Target source runtime routes first application through historical Owner/key and
tuple authority, then the Target Store batch. An unauthorized first application is persisted with both first
system and source-position results plus source accounting, without consuming Claim/Message/AttemptBudget or
writing Target quota deltas.

Focused command:
./gradlew test --tests com.nereusstream.delay.runtime.TargetQuotaStoreGateTest --tests com.nereusstream.delay.protocol.TargetPublishAdmissionBodyTest --tests com.nereusstream.delay.protocol.PublishAdmissionBodyTest --no-daemon --console=plain

Result: BUILD SUCCESSFUL; 24 tests total (TargetQuotaStoreGateTest 1/1, TargetPublishAdmissionBodyTest 4/4,
legacy PublishAdmissionBodyTest 19/19); zero skips, failures or errors. The RocksDB case verifies the rejected
first result, source position/frontier and absence of Message, Claim and AttemptBudget writes.

An earlier broader protocol run including ProtocolCodecTest had 69 tests with one unrelated existing failure:
stableCodeRegistryIsClosedAndRoundTripsEveryValue expects 108 StableCode values while this checkout has 109.
StableCode was not changed. No assertion was weakened or skipped.

./gradlew checkstyleTest checkstyleMain checkDocumentation --no-daemon --console=plain
Result: checkstyleTest passed. checkstyleMain failed only on existing 123-character lines in unmodified
PersistentLaneScheduler.java:1327 and TargetWorkerHostRuntime.java:377.

./gradlew checkDocumentation --no-daemon --console=plain
Result: BUILD SUCCESSFUL; package/implementation verifier suites (10 and 14 tests), historical closure and
documentation checks passed.

The original source lock above covered only the body/type/tuple and unauthorized rejection slice. The following
follow-up extends this evidence to an accepted first application through the actual RocksDB-backed Target Worker.

Follow-up source base: main@31e916693801fa2d440eb400644699ca4908e15a; changed source and test files are identified
by their SHA-256 values below.

Focused command:
./gradlew test --tests com.nereusstream.delay.runtime.TargetCommandStoreTest --no-daemon --console=plain

Result: BUILD SUCCESSFUL; TargetCommandStoreTest 5/5, zero skips, failures or errors. The new case starts from a
live replacement-Worker Claim and applies a signed type-13 Admission. The actual Store batch moves Message from
CLAIMED to PUBLISHING, removes Claim and ClaimCharge, stores an ADMITTED AttemptBudget, persists first system and
source-position results, and advances the source frontier. The test ACK callback returns UNKNOWN once; retry
ACKs the same pending entry without another Store mutation or RocksDB sequence. This is the Worker ACK-uncertainty
path with a deterministic test callback, not a real Broker/network response-loss test.
For DELIVERY_TIME_FIFO, the same case reads the committed OrderState and checks the exact PUBLISHING barrier,
matching ADMISSION_WATERMARK key, and absent serviceable head.

The regression exposed that legacy Target Control field discrimination decoded the v3 nested Owner field before
the type-13 route. Target Admission now routes before that legacy discriminator; old control routing and type-5
PUBLISH_ADMISSION v1 remain unchanged.

Scope limit: accepted Target Admission first application, Claim consumption and Worker ACK-unknown retry are now
verified on this actual RocksDB path. Production Owner/tuple-history authority assembly, real Broker ACK loss,
Target Outcome, missing-Claim recovery, Producer integration and central E5 validation remain open. E5 remains
IN_PROGRESS / PENDING_CENTRAL_VALIDATION.

